## Step 0 (owner gate, Sym side — mandatory before claiming (i) new)
The QSym side is NOT FOUND (2604.24903 BGST QGr has no Poincaré duality, cite it; 2009.13002 BMMN is the polynomial-ring analogue, adjacent).
Still owed: Gorenstein / apolar quotients of Sym, or rank of {s_{λ/μ}}_{|μ|=i}, for a ribbon or skew λ.
Search "span of skew Schur functions s_{λ/μ}", "skewing operators rank", and "Sym apolar Schur function".
If owned, cite it and make (ii) the theorem.

## Available tools
- Coproduct of ribbons in NSym (dual to F-multiplication = shuffle of descent classes); u^⊥ is adjoint to multiplication.
- Skew Schur / ribbon identities: r_α r_β = r_{α·β} + r_{α⊙β}; Jacobi–Trudi for ribbons.
- Ribbon equality: Billera–Thomas–van Willigenburg (math/0405434).
- Engines: `proofs/wake291/cap_rank.py` (brute force) and the fast engine in `proofs/wake291/gs/`.
- Gorenstein basics: QSym is commutative and Ann(R_s) is an ideal, so G_s is Artinian Gorenstein with 1-dim socle in degree d, and r_i = r_{d−i}.
  This is automatic: it is the rank of a matrix and of its transpose. The L=14 vector is symmetric, consistent.
  "Poincaré duality holds only for constant s" refers to the GLL cap product on H*(X_s): G_s is a PROPER quotient of H*(X_s) ≅ QSym/(GLL ideal) unless s is constant.
  Confirm this reading in hjv-scoping.md in 2 minutes. Do not chase an asymmetry.
- Symmetry halves the work: prove r_i = ⌊i/2⌋+1 for i ≤ d/2.
